<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1398–1338 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tye, Taia, Tiy, Tiyi, The Elder Lady · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiye was the Great Royal Wife of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> and grandmother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>. Her parents, Yuya and Thuya, were non-royal and came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Akhmim</a>, where Yuya was a wealthy landowner and priest. Married to Amenhotep III by the second year of his reign, she wielded great power during his reign and her son's: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, wrote to her directly. She outlived her husband by as many as twelve years; if she died soon after Akhenaten's 12th year (1338 BCE), she was born around 1398 BCE. In 2010 DNA analysis identified her as the Elder Lady, a mummy found in the tomb of Amenhotep II (KV35) in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiye.jpg" alt="Tiye" width="330" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Einsamer Schütze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ägyptisches_Museum_Berlin_027_detail_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tiye's father, Yuya, was a non-royal, wealthy landowner from the Upper Egyptian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Akhmim</a>, where he served as a priest and as superintendent of oxen or commander of the chariotry. Her mother, Thuya, held many titles in religious cults. Some Egyptologists have suggested that Yuya was of foreign origin, but a growing number of more recent scholars argue that Tiye and her parents were of indigenous Egyptian origin, since Yuya's name shows no phonological features characteristic of a foreign language. Her brother Anen was Second Prophet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>.</p><p>Tiye was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> by the second year of his reign. Commemorative scarabs announced her as Great Royal Wife and gave the names of her parents. Because the king was crowned as a child, scholars have proposed that a regency council formed around him, with Tiye's parents among its members. The couple had six confirmed children, among them Thutmose, crown prince and High Priest of Ptah, and Amenhotep IV, who succeeded his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> devoted several shrines to her and built a temple for her at Sedeinga in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, where she was worshipped as a form of Hathor-Tefnut. The American Egyptologists David O'Connor and Eric Cline note that no earlier queen figured so prominently in her husband's lifetime: she appears beside him in statuary, tomb and temple reliefs and stelae, and her name is paired with his on small objects and commemorative scarabs. She was even shown as a sphinx, an image formerly reserved for the king.</p><p>Tiye became her husband's trusted adviser and was known for her intelligence and strong personality. Foreign kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> were willing to deal with her directly, and she is regarded as the first Egyptian queen to have her name recorded on official acts. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> letter EA 26, Tushratta wrote to her to recall his good relations with her late husband and to ask for friendship with her son. She had a house at Akhetaten, her son's new capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In an inscription dated to the twelfth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s reign, about 1338 BCE, Tiye is mentioned for the last time, and she is thought to have died soon afterwards. She was probably first buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> and later moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>.</p><p>In 1898 Victor Loret found three mummies in a side chamber of the tomb of Amenhotep II, KV35. The older woman was called the Elder Lady, and several researchers argued that she was Tiye. In 2010, DNA analysis confirmed the identification.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queens such as <b>Tiye</b> and Nefertari had real influence, the God’s Wife of Amun was the highest priestess of Amun at Thebes, and Hatshepsut ruled as king.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Akhmim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Thebes, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.56,31.75&amp;year=-1359" aria-label="Akhmim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5387 1188 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tiye"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5837 1458h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5837 1458h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.56,31.75&amp;year=-1359">What was here in 1359 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
